--  Target-neutral I/O capabilities and their adapters.  The memory world
--  that provides them over the caller's storage is memory.ldn's, time is
--  core/time's, the network core/net's and the fakes of a system's
--  providers core/io/fake's; the hosted provider is hosted/system, selected
--  explicitly.

import core/mem
import core/text

--- The provider refused access to the requested file.
public no_access: atom
--- An I/O operation failed. Provider-specific diagnostic detail, if offered,
--- must be read from that provider.
public io_failed: atom
--- The path names a directory where a file was to be opened.
public is_a_directory: atom
--- The name a file or directory was to be made at is already taken.
public already_exists: atom

--- A directory entry that is a regular file.
public regular: atom
--- A directory entry that is itself a directory.
public subdirectory: atom
--- A directory entry that is a symbolic link, which a listing does not
--- follow.
public symbolic_link: atom
--- A directory entry that is anything else: a device, a pipe or a socket.
public special: atom
--- What a directory entry names: `regular`, `subdirectory`,
--- `symbolic_link` or `special`.
public entry_kind: type = regular | subdirectory | symbolic_link | special

--  A word wide enough for any provider's identifier: a POSIX descriptor,
--  a slot index, or a handle a system gives as a pointer.
file_value: type = distinct usize

--- Provider-defined file identifier. Use a handle only with the provider that
--- created it and obey that provider's close rules.
public file: type = file_value

--- Output capability. `write` writes every byte or fails, and a failure may
--- follow partial output. `write_some` makes one attempt: it reports
--- positive progress for nonempty input and zero for empty input, and a
--- failed attempt makes no progress.
---
--  Four narrow concepts and their composition: spec.md's D268 and D1952.
--- The capability is four narrow concepts over one provider type, so a
--- provider supplies only what it has: a UART is a writer and a read-only
--- image is a reader, while system and memory providers also supply files and
--- process entries. A consumer names the narrowest concept it uses;
--- diagnostics stream through any io.writer.  `world` composes all four for
--- roots that have everything.  A provider is a handle: a small copyable
--- value, passed by value, that refers to its state, so a program and its
--- logger share one without locking it.  An erased `any io.world` is built
--- from the address of a handle binding and dispatches every entry, and it
--- converts to an `any` of each concept it is built on: where an
--- `any io.writer` is wanted, pass the world (D1952).
public writer: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Write the entire byte slice or fail. Failure may follow partial
    --- output; do not blindly retry the whole slice.
    write: (self: provider, opened: file, bytes: []u8)
           -> none ! io_failed
    --- Attempt one transfer. Nonempty success returns positive progress,
    --- empty success returns zero, and failure transfers no bytes.
    write_some: (self: provider, opened: file, bytes: []u8)
                -> (count: usize) ! io_failed
end writer

--- Input capability. `read` puts at most `lenof into` bytes in the caller's
--- slice and returns the count; zero for a nonempty slice means end of
--- input. The slice's storage stays the caller's.
public reader: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Read at most the destination length and return progress; zero into a
    --- nonempty destination is end of input, and an empty one always reads
    --- zero.
    read: (self: provider, opened: file, into: []mut u8)
          -> (count: usize) ! io_failed
end reader

--- File lifecycle and identity capability. A path is an OS string, as
--- the system gives and takes it (`text.os_string`); a literal one is
--- `text.os_string_over("name")`. A successful open returns a
--- provider-specific handle; close consumes the handle even when reporting
--- failure.
public files: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Open a path for reading; return a handle owned by this provider.
    --- A path that names a directory is `is_a_directory`: a directory is
    --- read through `directories`.
    open_read: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
               -> (opened: file)
               ! mem.not_found | no_access | is_a_directory | io_failed
    --- Open a path for writing; creation and truncation follow the
    --- provider contract.  A path that names a directory is
    --- `is_a_directory`.
    open_write: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                -> (opened: file)
                ! mem.not_found | no_access | is_a_directory | io_failed
    --- Report whether two paths name the same file. The left path is an
    --- existing input: if it names no file the provider can examine, the
    --- call fails with `io_failed`. The right path may be an output not yet
    --- created: if it does not exist, the answer is false.
    same_file: (self: provider, left: text.os_string,
                right: text.os_string)
               -> (same: bool) ! io_failed
    --- Consume a file handle, including when closure reports a failure.
    close: (self: provider, sink opened: file)
           -> none ! io_failed
end files

--- Standard streams and argument access. An argument is an OS string
--- whose storage belongs to the provider; stream identifiers have
--- provider-specific lifetime and closing rules.
public process: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Return the standard-input handle, which a `reader` reads to end of
    --- input.
    input: (self: provider) -> (stream: file)
    --- Return the standard-output handle.
    out: (self: provider) -> (stream: file)
    --- Return the standard-error handle.
    err: (self: provider) -> (stream: file)
    --- Return the count of available arguments.
    arguments_length: (self: provider) -> (count: usize)
    --- Lend an argument by index; the provider retains its backing. Report
    --- mem.not_found when out of range.
    argument: (self: provider, index: usize)
              -> (value: text.os_string from self) ! mem.not_found
    --- Lend the program's name as the process was given it, C's
    --- `argv[0]`; the provider retains its backing.  Empty when none was
    --- given.
    program_name: (self: provider) -> (name: text.os_string from self)
end process

--- The process's environment: its variables, each a name and a value that
--- are OS strings whose storage belongs to the provider, in the order
--- the provider keeps them.  It is read-only: a process does not change
--- its own environment, which is not safe beside threads, and gives a
--- child the one it builds instead (`programs`).  It is a concept of its
--- own, outside `world`, so a provider that has no environment, and a
--- firmware image that never asks for one, carries none.
public environment: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Return the count of the environment's variables.
    variables_length: (self: provider) -> (count: usize)
    --- Lend the environment variable at an index; the provider retains its
    --- backing. Report mem.not_found when out of range.
    variable: (self: provider, index: usize)
              -> (item: text.os_variable from self) ! mem.not_found
    --- Lend the value of the first variable named `name`, compared as the
    --- system stores names, with no normalization. Report mem.not_found when
    --- none has that name.
    variable_named: (self: provider, name: text.os_string)
                    -> (value: text.os_string from self) ! mem.not_found
end environment

--- Composition of reader, writer, files and process over one provider type.
--- Consumers should request the narrowest capability they use.
--- `directories`, `environment`, `programs`, `clock`, `wall_clock`,
--- `entropy`, `network`, `file_system` and `terminals` are not part of it:
--- a program that lists a directory, reads its environment, runs another
--- program, reads the time, wants random bytes, uses the network, renames
--- a file or asks for a terminal names that concept as well.
public world: type = concept (provider: type) is reader, writer, files, process
end world

--- The fewest bytes a directory's buffer may have: room for any entry any
--- provider here gives, with its record and its terminator.  The hosted
--- provider refuses a smaller buffer when the directory is opened.
public directory_buffer: usize = 1024

--- An open directory: a value its caller keeps and edits, passing it
--- `inout` to `read_entry`, which refills `buffer` with entries as the
--- caller reads them.  The buffer is the caller's, so each entry's name is
--- lent from the directory and is good until the next read; copy a name to
--- keep it.  The other fields belong to the provider that opened it: its
--- own handle and stream for the directory, and how much of the buffer it
--- has filled and read.  Close it through the same provider.  A directory
--- dropped without closing keeps what its provider holds for it until the
--- process ends: there are no destructors, and a `defer` closes it on
--- every exit.
public directory: type = struct
    handle: usize
    stream: usize
    position: usize
    buffer: []mut u8
    filled: usize
    consumed: usize
    at_end: bool
end directory

--- One entry of a directory: its name, an OS string as the system stores
--- it, which need not be UTF-8, and what it is.  `read_entry` returns an
--- entry whose `at_end` is true at the end of the directory, its name then
--- empty; every other entry's `at_end` is false.
public entry: type = struct
    name: text.os_string
    kind: entry_kind
    at_end: bool
end entry

--- Directory listing and the names in a tree: open a directory and read its
--- entries, ask an entry's size, make and remove directories, and remove
--- files.  Paths are OS strings, as `files` takes them, and an entry's
--- name is one too, so a name read is given back unconverted.  A listing
--- leaves out `.` and `..`, which every directory holds and which are no
--- entries of its own.  It gives its entries in the system's order, and no
--- provider promises an order.
public directories: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Open the directory at `path` and read it into the caller's
    --- `buffer`.  A missing path and a path through or to something that
    --- is not a directory are `mem.not_found`.
    open_directory: (self: provider, path: text.os_string,
                     buffer: []mut u8)
                    -> (opened: directory from buffer)
                    ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed
    --- The directory's next entry, lent from it until the next read, or
    --- an entry whose `at_end` is true at its end.
    read_entry: (self: provider, inout opened: directory)
                -> (item: entry from opened) ! io_failed
    --- The size in bytes of the entry `name` in the open directory, not
    --- following a symbolic link.  A name no entry can have is
    --- `mem.not_found`: an empty one, one with a `/` or a NUL, or one longer
    --- than `directory_buffer` allows.  So is an entry removed since it was
    --- read.
    entry_size: (self: provider, opened: directory,
                 name: text.os_string)
                -> (size: u64) ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed
    --- Close the directory, which is consumed even when this fails.
    close_directory: (self: provider, sink opened: directory)
                     -> none ! io_failed
    --- Make a directory at `path`.  A path whose parent is missing is
    --- `mem.not_found`; a name already taken is `already_exists`.
    make_directory: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                    -> none ! already_exists | mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed
    --- Remove the file `path` names, not a directory.
    remove_file: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                 -> none ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed
    --- Remove the empty directory `path` names.
    remove_directory: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                      -> none ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed
end directories

--- Open a path for reading through `files` evidence. The caller must close
--- a successful handle using the same provider. A path that names a
--- directory fails `is_a_directory`.
public open_read: (provider: type is files, host: provider,
                   path: text.os_string)
                  -> (opened: file)
                  ! mem.not_found | no_access | is_a_directory | io_failed =
    opened = try provider.open_read(host, path)
end open_read

--- Open a path for writing through `files` evidence. Creation and
--- truncation behavior belongs to the provider; close a successful handle
--- through that provider. A path that names a directory fails
--- `is_a_directory`.
public open_write: (provider: type is files, host: provider,
                    path: text.os_string)
                   -> (opened: file)
                   ! mem.not_found | no_access | is_a_directory | io_failed =
    opened = try provider.open_write(host, path)
end open_write

--- Ask whether two paths name the same file. The provider applies its own
--- identity rules; a failure is `io_failed`.
public same_file: (provider: type is files, host: provider,
                   left: text.os_string, right: text.os_string)
                  -> (same: bool) ! io_failed =
    same = try provider.same_file(host, left, right)
end same_file

--- Close a provider handle. Treat it as consumed even if closing reports
--- `io_failed`; do not retry a consumed handle.
public close: (provider: type is files, host: provider,
               sink opened: file) -> none ! io_failed =
    try provider.close(host, opened)
end close

--- Open the directory at a path through `directories` evidence, reading it
--- into the caller's `buffer`; close a successful directory through the
--- same provider.
public open_directory: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                        path: text.os_string, buffer: []mut u8)
                       -> (opened: directory from buffer)
                       ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    opened = try provider.open_directory(host, path, buffer)
end open_directory

--- Read the directory's next entry, whose name is lent from the directory
--- until the next read; an entry whose `at_end` is true is the end of the
--- directory.
public read_entry: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                    inout opened: directory)
                   -> (item: entry from opened) ! io_failed =
    item = try provider.read_entry(host, opened)
end read_entry

--- The size in bytes of the entry `name` in an open directory, without
--- following a symbolic link.
public entry_size: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                    opened: directory, name: text.os_string)
                   -> (size: u64) ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    size = try provider.entry_size(host, opened, name)
end entry_size

--- Close a directory.  Treat it as consumed even if closing reports
--- `io_failed`.
public close_directory: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                         sink opened: directory) -> none ! io_failed =
    try provider.close_directory(host, opened)
end close_directory

--- Make a directory at a path.
public make_directory: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                        path: text.os_string)
                       -> none
                       ! already_exists | mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    try provider.make_directory(host, path)
end make_directory

--- Remove the file a path names.
public remove_file: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                     path: text.os_string)
                    -> none ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    try provider.remove_file(host, path)
end remove_file

--- Remove the empty directory a path names.
public remove_directory: (provider: type is directories, host: provider,
                          path: text.os_string)
                         -> none ! mem.not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    try provider.remove_directory(host, path)
end remove_directory

--- Read up to the destination length through `reader` evidence. Returns the
--- number of bytes placed in the buffer, or zero at end of input.
public read: (provider: type is reader, host: provider,
              opened: file, into: []mut u8)
             -> (count: usize) ! io_failed =
    count = try provider.read(host, opened, into)
end read

--- Write the complete byte slice through `writer` evidence. `io_failed` may
--- follow partial output; callers must not assume retrying the whole buffer
--- is safe.
public write: (provider: type is writer, host: provider,
               opened: file, bytes: []u8) -> none ! io_failed =
    try provider.write(host, opened, bytes)
end write

--  Where `write_format` hands its store: the file, through the writer's
--  handle.
file_outlet: type (provider: type) = struct
    host: provider
    opened: file
end file_outlet

file_take: (provider: type is writer, self: ptr mut file_outlet(provider),
            bytes: []u8) -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    provider.write(self.val.host, self.val.opened, bytes) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        fail mem.out_of_memory
    end
end file_take

(provider: type is writer) file_outlet(provider) is text.outlet
    (take: file_take)

--  The bytes `write_format` formats into on the stack before it writes
--  them: a line no longer than this is one `write`.
format_store: usize = 128

--- Write `template` to the file with each `{}` replaced by the next of
--- `values`, as `text.format` writes them: `io.write_format(host,
--- io.out(host), "{} of {}\n", [any(addr done), any(addr total)])`.  The
--- caller supplies no buffer and has nothing to flush: the text is formatted
--- into 128 bytes on the stack, which are written whenever they fill and at
--- the end.  So a line that fits is one write, and a value of any length is
--- written in pieces.  The template is checked against its values first, so
--- `text.invalid_format` writes nothing.  A failed write is `io_failed` and
--- may follow partial output, as `write`'s may.  A conformance of the
--- program's own that asks for more room at once than the 128 bytes, or
--- right-aligns a piece of a value longer than them that was already written,
--- is `io_failed` too, since its text cannot be written.  The values are
--- `text.formattable`'s types; floats are not formattable yet.  For many
--- writes to one file, a `buffered_writer` gathers them into fewer.
public write_format: (provider: type is writer, host: provider,
                      opened: file, template: utf8,
                      values: []any text.formattable)
                     -> none ! io_failed | text.invalid_format =
    mut store: [format_store]u8 = zeroed
    mut target: file_outlet(provider) = (host: host, opened: opened)
    onto: any text.outlet = any(addr target)
    text.format_onto(store[0..<format_store], onto, template, values)
        else (problem)
        fail text.invalid_format when problem == text.invalid_format
        fail io_failed
    end
end write_format

--- Make one write attempt and return its progress. Nonempty success is
--- positive; empty input returns zero; failure transfers no bytes.
public write_some: (provider: type is writer, host: provider,
                    opened: file, bytes: []u8) -> (count: usize) ! io_failed =
    count = try provider.write_some(host, opened, bytes)
end write_some

--- Return the provider's standard-input identifier. Read it through
--- `reader` evidence; zero from a nonempty read is end of input.
public input: (provider: type is process, host: provider)
              -> (stream: file) =
    stream = provider.input(host)
end input

--- Return the provider's standard-output identifier.
public out: (provider: type is process, host: provider)
            -> (stream: file) =
    stream = provider.out(host)
end out

--- Return the provider's standard-error identifier.
public err: (provider: type is process, host: provider)
            -> (stream: file) =
    stream = provider.err(host)
end err

--- Return the number of arguments after the program's name, which is not
--- one of them: C's `argc - 1`.
public arguments_length: (provider: type is process, host: provider)
                         -> (count: usize) =
    count = provider.arguments_length(host)
end arguments_length

--- Lend the process argument at `index` as an OS string; index zero is the
--- first argument after the program's name, C's `argv[1]`. An index that
--- names no argument reports `mem.not_found`. The provider's argument backing
--- must outlive the string's use. Convert it to `utf8` with the checked
--- `text.from_os_string`, or take its bytes with `text.os_string_bytes`;
--- passed on as a path, it is not converted at all.
public argument: (provider: type is process, host: provider, index: usize)
                 -> (value: text.os_string from host) ! mem.not_found =
    value = try provider.argument(host, index)
end argument

--- Lend the name the program was run by, as whoever started it gave it:
--- C's `argv[0]`, which `argument` never returns. It is often the path the
--- program was started by and may be any string its parent chose, a bare
--- name, a relative path or none at all, so it is the name to write in a
--- usage line or to describe the program by, not proof of where its file
--- is. It is empty when the parent gave no name. Its backing is the
--- provider's, as an argument's is.
public program_name: (provider: type is process, host: provider)
                     -> (name: text.os_string from host) =
    name = provider.program_name(host)
end program_name

--- Return the number of variables in the provider's environment.
public variables_length: (provider: type is environment, host: provider)
                         -> (count: usize) =
    count = provider.variables_length(host)
end variables_length

--- Lend the environment variable at `index`, from zero, its name and value
--- OS strings.  Reports `mem.not_found` for an index at or past the count;
--- the provider's backing must outlive its use.  Every index below
--- `variables_length` gives one, so a loop over them lists the whole
--- environment.
public variable: (provider: type is environment, host: provider,
                  index: usize)
                 -> (item: text.os_variable from host) ! mem.not_found =
    item = try provider.variable(host, index)
end variable

--- Lend the value of the environment variable named `name`, the first one
--- when the environment names it twice.  Reports `mem.not_found` when no
--- variable has that name.  `text.from_os_string` is the value's checked
--- conversion to `utf8`.
public variable_named: (provider: type is environment, host: provider,
                        name: text.os_string)
                       -> (value: text.os_string from host)
                       ! mem.not_found =
    value = try provider.variable_named(host, name)
end variable_named
